# hub-database environment - the SCHEMA for hub-database/.env.
#
# The file is named environment.example rather than .env.example. Every hub stack follows the
# same convention; see hub-ops/environment.example for why the name changed.
#
# WHERE THE REAL VALUES LIVE FOR A REBUILD: Barbican.
#   ../../infra/control_plane/secrets.tf reads the LOCAL hub-database/.env at plan time and stores it
#   as the <instance>-db-env secret. cloud-init fetches that payload back to
#   /opt/clinicalomxdb/hub-database/.env on every boot of a fresh instance.
#   To rotate a value: change it in the local .env, then tofu apply in ../../infra/control_plane, then
#   re-fetch on the VM. Editing the file on the VM alone is lost on the next rebuild.
#
#   cloud-init still APPENDS GHCR_USERNAME and COMX_CI_READ to the fetched payload. Neither is
#   read by anything in this stack any more: the image build stopped git-cloning clinicalomx
#   when that pin moved to the index, and the two keys went with it. They are harmless extras
#   on a VM built before cloud-init drops the append, which is why they are not listed below.
#
# Consumed by the database, pgadmin and python-lamindb services through env_file, and by compose
# interpolation for the database service's three host-path binds, the explorer service and the
# image build. An interpolated key is needed before any container exists, so a missing one fails
# the render rather than the container.
# `make db-env-check` fails if the real .env is missing any key below.

# --- config (non-secret) -----------------------------------------------------
# CONFIG: Postgres superuser role, and the database created on first boot. Also read by
# hub-database/scripts/wait_for_postgres.sh for its pg_isready probe.
POSTGRES_USER=root
POSTGRES_DB=lamindb

# CONFIG: pgAdmin's own login account. The password below is the other half of it.
PGADMIN_DEFAULT_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# --- host paths: the three settings that differ per machine -------------------
# DB_CERT_DIR, DB_DATA_DIR and LAMIN_HOME. The first two default to a gitignored repo-local
# directory. LAMIN_HOME defaults to a directory of this project's own OUTSIDE the working tree,
# because the connect writes a real LAMIN_API_KEY and instance settings carrying the Postgres URI
# with its password into it: a working tree is synced, backed up and archived in ways a directory
# outside it is not, and not being committable is not the same as not being exposed. Its own line
# below says why the default is not your ~/.lamin. `make db-up` creates all three, so a fresh clone
# comes up with no sudo and no mkdir either way.
# The VM overrides all three with its own paths; cloud-init appends those to .env after
# fetching the Barbican payload, because that payload is a copy of a developer's local file and so
# carries these dev values. A later assignment wins in an --env-file, which is what makes the
# append work. None has a default in docker-compose.yml, and no bind may create its own host
# path: a default would let a host missing the key come up on a directory beside the repo, and
# host-path creation would turn a typo into an empty directory that looks healthy. Neither covers
# the VM's data disk failing to mount, because that mountpoint exists either way; the DB_DATA_DIR
# mount in docker-compose.yml says why and names the guard that would.
#
# A value is absolute, or relative to hub-database/, or starts with ~ for your home directory.
# Compose resolves a bind source after parsing this file as dotenv, so it strips surrounding
# quotes and a space-preceded inline comment and expands a leading ~, none of which a shell
# reading the raw line does. hub-database/scripts/host_path.sh is the one place those rules are
# implemented for the make targets, so what they create is what compose binds.
#
# A repo-local value also has to stay out of the lamindb image build context, which is this
# directory; hub-database/.dockerignore covers the two repo-local defaults below, plus the old
# in-tree LAMIN_HOME default, and says why.

# CONFIG: the directory holding server.crt and server.key, the pair Postgres serves TLS with.
# The VM sets /opt/certificates, generated by cloud-init one level above the repo. On a dev box
# `make db-up` generates a self-signed pair into the default below if one is not already there.
# It generates them in a container, so both end up owned by uid 999 with the key at 600, which
# is what Postgres demands of a key file and what a developer cannot arrange without sudo.
# Removing them therefore also needs docker or sudo; deleting the pair is how you rotate it.
DB_CERT_DIR=./certs

# CONFIG: where the Postgres cluster lives on THIS host, bind-mounted at /var/lib/postgresql.
# Relative values are resolved against hub-database/, not your CWD. The VM sets
# /mnt/data/postgres, its mounted data disk.
DB_DATA_DIR=./pgdata

# CONFIG: host path bind-mounted at /root/.lamin in the explorer AND python-lamindb services, so
# every container reads the same connected instance instead of logging in for itself. The VM sets
# /home/ubuntu/.lamin, written by cloud-init.
#
# This is what makes a connect stick. Which instance is connected is per-container state, and the
# schema-* verbs each start a NEW container with `compose run --rm`, so without a shared host
# directory a connection made by one is invisible to the next. The default below is empty until you
# run a connect, and the Explore tab reports no instance until then. Off the VM that connect is
# `make lamin-local`, because the hub instance `make lamin-connect` writes names a database at an
# address a developer machine cannot route to; on the VM it is `make lamin-connect`.
#
# The default is PROJECT-SCOPED, and outside the working tree, which is two separate decisions.
#
# Outside the tree because what the connect writes here is credentials: LAMIN_API_KEY and the
# instance settings holding the Postgres URI with its password. A working tree is synced, backed up
# and archived in ways a directory outside it is not, and not being committable is not the same as
# not being exposed. `make lamin-connect` also leaves the directory owner-only, so it is not
# world-readable wherever it lands.
#
# Project-scoped rather than your own ~/.lamin because ~/.lamin is YOUR lamindb settings directory,
# holding whatever instance you last connected on this host, and the schema-* verbs WRITE. With
# ~/.lamin as the default, one `make schema-apply` run before `make lamin-connect` would reconcile
# this project's foundation vocabulary into an unrelated instance and exit zero. A default must not
# be able to write into someone else's instance, and a directory of this project's own removes that
# by construction rather than by warning about it.
#
# Sharing your host connection with this project is therefore an OPT-IN: set LAMIN_HOME=~/.lamin
# here deliberately, knowing the schema-* verbs then write into whatever that directory names.
#
# DO NOT RUN THESE TARGETS UNDER SUDO, and this is the key that makes it matter. A leading ~
# resolves against the INVOKING user's home in both resolvers: host_path.sh substitutes $HOME, and
# compose expands the tilde with os.UserHomeDir(). None of these targets needs sudo, which is why
# every default here is chosen so a fresh clone needs neither sudo nor mkdir. Under sudo, HOME
# becomes /root, so `sudo make db-up` creates and binds /root/.clinicalomxdb/lamin while a later
# plain `make schema-review` resolves your own, and the two invocations are looking at different
# directories. The symptom does not say so: the surface reports NO INSTANCE CONNECTED, which points
# at the connection when the actual cause is the path. That hazard is about PATH RESOLUTION, and it
# is the only one here that follows the invoking user. OWNERSHIP is a separate matter and does not:
# the chown on lamin-connect takes its uid from the owner of the directory ABOVE LAMIN_HOME, so
# every invocation shape lands on the same account.
#
# THIS MUST NAME A LEAF, not a directory you also keep other things in. lamin-connect chmods and
# chowns it RECURSIVELY, so hub-database/scripts/lamin_home_owner.sh refuses three shapes before
# the connect runs and names the resolved path when it does: a bare ~ or any other value landing on
# your home directory, the filesystem root, and anything whose parent is owned by root, which is
# what /home/<user> and most system paths resolve to. `make lamin-connect` stops rather than
# rewriting a whole home directory.
#
# Moving from an earlier default: change the line to the value below, re-run `make db-up` and then
# whichever connect you use, `make lamin-connect` or `make lamin-local`, then DELETE the directory
# you came from, whether that is the in-tree
# `hub-database/lamin/` or a `~/.lamin` this project populated. Either one holds a real
# LAMIN_API_KEY and a database password, so it is not something to leave behind. An unchanged .env
# keeps working meanwhile, because every reader of this value resolves it the same way: the bind,
# db-dirs which creates it, db-nuke which prints it, and lamin-connect which takes the chown target
# from its parent. Retargeting it therefore needs a `make db-up`, so db-dirs creates the new path
# before the bind refuses to.
#
# The schema-* targets still accept it on the command line, which wins: make exports a
# command-line variable and compose prefers the environment over --env-file.
LAMIN_HOME=~/.clinicalomxdb/lamin

# CONFIG: the Explore tab's Python console (hub-database/explorer). Set to off to serve only the
# no-eval query builder. The console is NOT sandboxed and evaluates Python in the container;
# ADR 0007 makes it admin-only, and until that lands reaching the portal already implies ssh on
# this host.
EXPLORER_CONSOLE=on

# CONFIG: the database host that hub-database/scripts/lamin_connect.sh rewrites the connected
# instance's settings to. The connect reads it from this file, so neither host needs an operator to
# remember an argument. The two correct values are not the same:
#   dev box:          EMPTY, as below, which skips the rewrite and keeps whatever LaminHub registered
#   control-plane VM: postgres, the container name on clinicalomxdb_net, because the public host
#                     LaminHub registered is DEAD from on the box and its own Postgres is a
#                     container away
# So the empty line below is the DEV value. Copying it onto a VM is wrong, and wrong quietly: the
# connect exits zero having pointed the Explore tab, the review app and the schema-* verbs at a
# database none of them can reach.
#
# A correctly provisioned VM is already protected without anyone reading this. cloud-init appends
# LAMIN_DB_HOST_OVERRIDE=postgres to the VM's .env AFTER the Barbican payload, and a later
# assignment wins in an --env-file, so the empty value this file contributes to that payload is
# overridden rather than obeyed.
#
# `make db-env-check` catches this key being ABSENT and nothing more; it compares keys, so an empty
# value passes. The Makefile comment at lamin-connect says what that leaves uncovered.
#
# The hint below is what db-env-check prints above this key when it is missing. It exists because
# the check otherwise prints the example value and says to append it, and this is the one key whose
# example value is the DEV one; an operator appending it on a VM would pass the re-run and then get
# a connect that silently writes the dead public host.
# A hint is ONE line: the regex matches a single line, so a continuation would be silently dropped.
# env-check-hint LAMIN_DB_HOST_OVERRIDE: on a control-plane VM append postgres, not the empty dev-box value shown below (../../infra/control_plane/README.md)
LAMIN_DB_HOST_OVERRIDE=

# --- secrets (never commit a real value) -------------------------------------
# SECRET: Postgres superuser password. It guards port 5432, which is published and reachable
# from the facility's SHARED translation host, so this is the most exposed value in the repo.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGEME

# SECRET: pgAdmin's login password. pgAdmin is loopback-only, reached at /pgadmin/ through the
# operator tunnel, so this guards it against anyone who already has ssh on the VM.
PGADMIN_DEFAULT_PASSWORD=CHANGEME

# SECRET: LaminHub API key, from your LaminHub account settings. `lamin login` reads it from the
# environment; without it no LaminDB operation works at all.
LAMIN_API_KEY=CHANGEME

# --- deliberately NOT listed here --------------------------------------------
# POSTGRES_IP was removed. It was carried here and in the retired root example, but nothing in
# the repo reads it: no compose file interpolates it, no script and no service. Postgres is
# addressed by the clinicalomxdb_postgres network alias, and lamindb gets its connection from
# LaminHub. A key nobody reads is a key a rotation can miss.
